From cc7597777c4adc87b97a4edda76a4c53cd5371c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roger Luethi <luethi@osism.tech>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:43:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cron: rotate HAProxy logs by renaming them

The global logrotate configuration rotates every log with copytruncate:
logrotate copies the file to .1 and then truncates the original. The
copy needs free space equal to the size of the log. When the log is
larger than the free space, the copy fails with ENOSPC, logrotate
removes the partial copy and leaves the original untouched. The log
never shrinks, and each daily run fills the disk to 100% while it
copies. This is the state a host reaches after its HAProxy log has
grown unrotated for a long time, and the state in which rotation is
needed most.

copytruncate exists for writers that keep their log file open. The
HAProxy log is not written by such a process. HAProxy sends its log
via syslog to fluentd, and fluentd's file output writes
/var/log/kolla/haproxy/haproxy_latest.log with append enabled, which
opens the file, appends a chunk and closes it again on every flush
(File.open with a block in out_file.rb). The haproxy container does not
mount kolla_logs. Nothing else holds the file open, so after a rename
fluentd creates a new file on its next flush.

Use nocopytruncate for the HAProxy logs. Renaming needs no free space,
so even an oversized log is rotated on the first run, and the space
needed for a rotation no longer depends on the size of the log.

Signed-off-by: Roger Luethi <luethi@osism.tech>
---
 ansible/roles/cron/templates/cron-logrotate-haproxy.conf.j2 | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 ansible/roles/cron/templates/cron-logrotate-haproxy.conf.j2

diff --git a/ansible/roles/cron/templates/cron-logrotate-haproxy.conf.j2 b/ansible/roles/cron/templates/cron-logrotate-haproxy.conf.j2
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..a9dead996
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ansible/roles/cron/templates/cron-logrotate-haproxy.conf.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,4 @@
+"/var/log/kolla/haproxy/*.log"
+{
+    nocopytruncate
+}
